 while  thinking what it actually means for a  dungeon to be successful I managed to  narrow down my criteria to a list of 5  dungeon mechanics and items complexity  and or survival aesthetics and  atmosphere which also includes music  enemies and bosses and size and scope so  let's start from the  and just work our way down starting with  dungeon mechanics and items oh and here  to help me with this analysis is  Nintendo black crisis  what would a traditional dungeon in  breadth of the wild to look like  as stated before completely going back  to the old formula is likely not going  to work a room like this would be a  cakewalk for breadth of the wilds link  given the fact that he is much more  mobile than his past iterations unless  of course they take away these tools  once you enter the dungeon removing the  paraglider for example similar to how  mario sunshine removes flood from the  equation when entering certain  platforming levels but this option isn't  preferred as it's a huge missed  opportunity to create brand new dungeon  experiences and ideas the way the  shrines and the divine beast were  structured required link to lose his  ability to climb walls which is  understandable since it would make it  too easy to get around a lot of puzzles  presented hence climate will service 'as  in these structures are limited to  grilles and ladders link is basically  set back to the restrictions of the  previous games which only allow climbing  on designated surfaces however there are  plenty of ways to traverse a dungeon  with the freeform climbing mechanic in  mind Nintendo can still restrict  climbing on specific surfaces by making  them slippery or knock you off by means  of electric tiles or spiked surfaces  certain puzzles can be solely based on  how you're going to reach a certain door  switch or platform but let's switch back  to the paraglider for a moment there are  a couple of shrines in breadth of the  wild that demonstrate how effective the  paraglider is at granting the player  never-before-seen options for dungeon  traversal I want to present to you the  potential of verticality in the past  dungeons have used the aspect of a  vertical space to shape a dungeons  layout but never before have we had  access to the type of mobility found in  breadth of the wild meaning the dungeons  can be vastly bigger and more impressive  which is one of breath of the wild  strong points even in the overworld just  the sheer magnitude and size of certain  landmarks alone can be overwhelming for  the most part platforming has been  reserved for the 2d installments the  auto jump in the 3d games takes away the  demand for precise jumping as you  we hold the control stick in the right  direction and you are good to go  the platforms are all spaced out so that  the jump will rarely fail and in the  words of Aaron Hanson why do I got a  leap across these platforms there's no  challenge here I hold the joystick up  just because the scenarios treacherous  doesn't mean the game is actually  treacherous and that is exactly why  breath of the wild 2 has a great  opportunity to introduce complex  platforming into the 3d games with  dungeons that require all sorts of crazy  stunts to parkour your way around here  are a couple of suggestions how about  jumping from wall to wall or climbing a  wall while obstacles are swaying back  and forth and you have to time your  jumps to avoid getting knocked off  climbing walls or blocks that move and  you have to cross from one to another at  the right time or better yet introduce  an item like the hookshot that allows  you to hook onto a wall while gliding  through the air cling to the walls or  prematurely release the chain to get an  extra speed boost to make your way  across an obstacle reminiscent of Just  Cause which combine paragliding with the  grappling hook to gain extra speed the  speaking of items dungeon items should  return in some shape or form one of the  most fun aspects of the traditional  dungeons in this series was the fact  that a significant portion of the  dungeon was inaccessible until you found  the dungeons main item in this regard  this item was basically functioning as  key to unlock new sections of the  dungeon and a great way to force  backtracking and making a mental note of  certain rooms you were not able to cross  previously breadth of the wild made  these options impossible by giving you  all the necessary tools at the very  start of the game not only that it  sabotages its own challenges because in  a lot of ways the player has an easier  way around a certain obstacle this  particular shrine called wind guides  located at the western border of the map  demonstrates the possibilities but also  the problems a sequel has to overcome  not only is this shrine bigger than most  other shrines but also shows how  verticality can be implemented in  breadth of the wild - you enter this  first room where you simply need to  activate this fan using a cork leaf  which you can actually get from this  chest right here next you enter this  area where you use the aforementioned  Corrick leaf to propel yourself to this  ledge  after which you see the sheikah monk but  are denied access by a locked door one  of only a few locked doors found in  of the wild following the path forward  presents you with this huge vertical  room which is traversed using all kinds  of different methods after playing  through so many small shrines this room  alone was a pleasant surprise  this also presents a problem however  you're supposed to fan an explosive  barrel across the gap to blow up these  stone blocks but if you happen to have  bomb arrows in your arsenal this puzzle  can be completely ignored and simply one  arrow will suffice to get the job done a  good example of how birth of the wilds  puzzles can be neglected by having the  right items in your stash you can ignore  certain fire based puzzles by having  fire based weapons I your disposal or  cheat these electricity puzzles by  dropping metal weapons and shields to  guide the current you get the point now  it is really cool how you can solve  puzzles in different ways something  that's actually rare in the Zelda  franchise usually there is only one  predetermined solution but it's much  preferred to outsmart a puzzle with  creative thinking rather than to  coincidentally carry an item that  destroys the purpose of the puzzle like  for instance in this shrine right here  where you're supposed to guide this  orbital on a path of drafts provided by  these fans  the idea is to rush to this block while  the orb gets guided along the path you  simply wait for the orb to reach the  pedestal and there you go but you can  simply place the orb right next to the  pedestal and use this metal box that is  meant to block a specific fans of the  orb crosses correctly to simply push the  orb in the pedestal from afar this is  off script but I actually solved it a  bit differently I place the orb down at  then used stasis ran to the block and  then just waited for the stasis to stop  these methods make you feel rather  clever by basically cheating the system  blue flame is another shrine that stands  out like wind guides it's a lot bigger  than most other shrines the central  mechanic behind this shrine is to  transfer this blue flame from section to  section the fact that the floor is lava  and you can't use a paraglider while  carrying the flame serves as a great way  to limit the player without a feeling  force all-in-all we think that some of  these ideas combined with new potential  mechanics that can be introduced in the  sequel could make breath of the wild to  dungeons work in many different ways  which brings us to the next point on the  list complexity and/or survival the  reason why there's  slash right here is because a dungeon  doesn't always have to meet both  criteria at the same time to succeed in  my book some are complex on a mechanical  level brainteaser if you will while  others derive their challenge from an  endurance round of combat and traps and  other obstacles that drain your hearts  with every step forward also known as a  gauntlet introduced by Mark Brown in his  boss key series yet others fall  somewhere in between in my opinion the  best sets of dungeons in the Zelda game  are the ones that mixes it up between  different styles a tangible comparison  would be the water temple versus the  shadow temple in ocarina of time the  shadow temple gets a lot of flack for  being painfully linear but I think that  was exactly the point  instead of getting lost and forcing you  to understand its architecture it's an  endurance round to the finish by the  time you reach the final stretch of the  dungeon you have descended deeper and  deeper into its inner structure you're  actively trying to stay alive since the  punishment is having to walk all the way  back to where you left off the water  temple is the complete opposite the  enemies and combat challenges are  nothing to write home about and if you  still manage to die then getting back to  the place where you left off doesn't  really take a whole lot of time instead  it's the complex structure it's many  floors different water levels and  interconnected rooms that drives the  challenge for breath the while to  presenting a gauntlet challenge isn't  too difficult especially if they  introduce some new and challenging enemy  types a dungeon like this would mostly  consist of narrow caves or corridors or  closed off rooms that just keep going  forward and forward rooms filled with  traps and enemies that provide a decent  challenge and you're good to go  even more so if progress is halted until  you defeat every last enemy in a room or  area something that was exceptionally  rare in breadth the wild as in most  cases you don't really have to fight  anything if you don't want to and can  just circumnavigate the enemies  altogether in terms of complexity  however there's plenty of options to  choose from and to a certain extent the  divine beasts as well as a small handful  of shrines already toyed around with  some great ideas I just wish they would  last a little bit longer the divine  beasts all incorporate the same general  idea of manipulating the dungeon itself  with your chica slate in my very first  playthrough once I entered the second  divine beast  it immediately dawned on me that this is  basically the same concept in the sequel  dungeon centered around this puzzle box  motif would be a lot more enjoyable if  they introduced a unique central  mechanic that doesn't revolve around the  same idea one of the aspects that made  dungeons in say Majora's Mask so  effective was the fact that each one had  a unique mechanic that requires an  understanding of how the dungeon works  before you are ready to progress by the  time you beat the second divine beasts  you already know what to expect from the  last to get the map manipulate sections  of the dungeon activated terminals fight  the boss it doesn't leave much room for  surprises next let's take a look at  aesthetics and atmosphere this part will  be a short one as it kind of speaks for  itself  the big turndown for both the divine  beasts and the shrines was the fact that  it didn't provide much variety don't get  us wrong the general idea of the orange  and blue technological vibe in the  shrines was kinda cool and as a  standalone dungeon it would have been a  great fit its kind of similar to the  temple of time in Twilight Princess a  very sterile and technological  atmosphere but after 120 iterations yeah  it starts to get old the same thing goes  for the divine beasts which all have  this brownish looking sheikah design the  music is slightly different in each one  but not by much it is really cool how  the music will ramp up with each  terminal you activate similar to Hyrule  Castle and Twilight Princess where the  music slowly gets more intense as you  progress however those fitting themes  found throughout the previous games are  greatly missed the haunting melody of  the forest temple the serene eastern  vibe of the ancient cistern and the  bizarre floaty theme in the city in the  sky from the very first 3d title every  dungeon had its own distinct look and  feel we don't necessarily need to go  back to the overused forest fire water  themes from before there are plenty of  other options to go with deep dark  caverns a dungeon based around  electricity and old abandoned ruin  filled with Indiana Jones esque traps or  a dungeon shaped like a huge tower that  is all about vertical movement where it  just keeps going up and up until you  reached a slight heights similar to the  Snowhead temple with this new engine  dungeons could be stretched to their  fullest potential a mix between the new  idea  from breath of the wild and the more  traditional 3d dungeons designed from  the older games with that in mind let's  take a look at something that definitely  changed from the older games namely  enemies and bosses when talking about  breath the wilds dungeons and mentioning  bosses and enemies we all know what I'm  about to say the dungeon bosses in  breath the wild were uninspired to say  the least they all share the same  aesthetics and generally felt like a  copy-paste designing including calamity  Ganon himself and I have to ask with a  game that has climbing as one of its  central mechanics why was this rarely  ever used in boss fights with the  exception of Stone Tallis to a very  small degree the fact that climbing was  not utilized further is a mystery to me  Shadow of the Colossus and God of War  set the perfect example in that regard  of course not every boss has to be  fought this way but come on when the  inclusion of climbing in breadth the  wild was first revealed in 2016 trailer  large and epic boss fights with climbing  involved was one of the first things I  imagined the sequel has a chance to  rectify this climbing could be used in  so many ways to battle a boss not just  climbing the body of the boss itself but  also your surroundings to get a vantage  point to hit it from above for example  all the while the boss could try to  knock you off with physical attacks or  projectiles forcing you to make time  jumps to avoid it now I can't say for  sure if we're gonna start with a fully  upgraded stamina wheel or if we're gonna  have to start from scratch but either  way not having enough stamina could  simply mean that you are not yet ready  to fight a specific boss until you  upgrade your stamina wheel which in  return encourages exploration to grow  stronger similar to a game like Dark  Souls where you won't even stand a  chance against a certain boss until you  level up first and again something like  a hook shot or a similar type of device  could be used in combination with  climbing and paragliding to latch on to  either the boss itself or an object like  a grill very reminiscent of a fight with  our garage in Twilight Princess but now  with a lot more mobility options the  closest we ever came to an actual aerial  boss battle was to fight with the curse  nhadra atop mount lanayru-- which now  that I think about it didn't even really  fight back huh not even sure if I would  classify  this as a boss battle but anyway an idea  like this could be expanded upon instead  of simply picking up some eye weak  points with arrows how about actually  having to land on the giant behemoth and  wailing on it that would be awesome  I'm pretty sure that Nintendo can come  up with some very unique looking  creatures and creative ways to fight  them as they have done so in the past  now that Lincoln climb and Glide bosses  can be bigger and more epic than ever  before oh and by the way mini bosses  would also make a welcome return as for  the rest of the dungeon enemies well  yeah the divine beast might as well have  not included them at all some guardian  Scouts here and they're flying curse  skulls and these eyeball things  definitely a huge step back from the  previous titles which all featured many  different enemy encounters and smart  enemy placement all around not only that  but in previous games each dungeon hosts  a set of enemies that are relevant to  its general theme undead and creepy  enemies mechanical enemies water-based  enemies and bats lots and lots of  fricking bats  nobody likes fighting these Nintendo in  any game so please don't  anyway let's wrap things up with the  final part of the list  size and scope the divine beasts didn't  exactly last very long and if a dungeon  is too short it undermines the  experience of course the same thing goes  the other way around if a dungeon is  Toulon and seems to drag on forever it  will eventually start to become  monotonous having a balanced dungeon  length is crucial a gigantic and  beautifully designed central room that  unlocks bit by bit for instance could be  amazing especially if combined with the  things we talked about in the previous  sections where you need to scale a  massive room solve puzzles to open  locked doors and challenging platform  sections involving the paraglider and  climbing if Nintendo indeed goes back to  more traditional dungeons I'm not too  worried that they would mess this part  up as judging by the track record they  have generally managed to make balanced  dungeons that were not over in a flash  but also didn't drag on too long just  keep in mind with breadth of the wild  size no longer poses the same problem a  massive room with sky high ceilings will  be much easier to traverse than it ever  in previous games it's more about how  long a dungeon is willing to go on for  before it starts to get stale just a  final stray thought I know a lot of work  went into the overworld of breadth the  wild as well as the physics and  chemistry engine I cannot even imagine  the amount of hours that went into  constructing this world and perfecting  the mechanics but now that all of this  exists and for a large part can be  reused it would be nice to have more  than just four main dungeons this time  around I don't want to sound demanding  but having the same amount of dungeons  as Majora's Mask a game that was  developed in just one year was kind of a  letdown for a game this ambitious and I  know breadth the wilds focus wasn't on  the dungeons this time around but rather  the overworld but now that we have  explored this landscape so extensively  it would be best if the overworld gets a  little bit more of a backseat this time  around you will still be able to roam  around and mined for materials do quests  and explore these beautiful locations  all that good stuff but have the main  focus be on something that lies beneath  the surface under the waters or maybe  even high up in the sky or on a remote  island somewhere this will also make  sure that the sequel will stand out  against this precursor something that is  always very hard for a direct sequel  either it turns out way too similar and  thus an original or strays away so far  from the original that it's  unrecognizable as a direct sequel and  that pretty much sums it up I want to  personally thank Nintendo black crisis  for joining me in this video I owe you  one  a link to his channel will of course be  in the description I will go into more  specifics in the near future as we  haven't even scratched the surface when  it comes to the possibilities look at  this as more of an introduction or  general idea of how a dungeon could be  constructed for the sequel  if breadth the wall to will for instance  bring back underwater swimming that  would already open up so many new  dungeon ideas if you made it all the way  through then you have just watched my  very first video and I thank you for  that I created this channel out of  passion for dungeon design and level  design in general however I will not shy  away from covering other topics in the  future and not only restricted to the  Zelda franchise and finally it's pretty  much youtube law to state this at the  end so here we go light  comment and subscribe until the next  video this is Don signing off and have a  good one okay now how do I stop reported

